<svelte:head><title>Global Pay — Yasshmita Naicker</title><meta name="description" content="A customer-led approach to cross-border payments. A Global Pay UX and design leadership case study by Yasshmita Naicker."/></svelte:head>
<main id="main"><section class="case-hero section-pad" id="top"><a class="back-link" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">CASE STUDY 01 / CROSS-BORDER REMITTANCE</p><h1>Global Pay<span>Make complexity<br/><em>feel simple.</em></span></h1><p class="case-deck">A guided experience that helps customers send money internationally with confidence.</p><div class="case-meta"><div><span>ROLE</span>Product Designer<br/>Design Lead responsibilities</div><div><span>CHANNELS</span>App + Desktop</div><div><span>DURATION</span>2 years</div><div><span>FOCUS</span>UX, journey mapping,<br/>testing & service design</div></div></section><div class="case-image"><img src="/images/experience.webp" alt="Global Pay experience across a desktop and two mobile devices"/></div><section class="case-body section-pad"><div class="case-row"><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE CONTEXT</p><div><h2>A faster way to send.<br/><em>A clearer way forward.</em></h2><p>Customers could previously send money abroad through Western Union or SWIFT payments, which took several working days to clear. The opportunity was to create a faster, more convenient and more flexible cross-border payment experience.</p></div></div><div class="case-row"><p class="eyebrow">02 / THE CHALLENGE</p><div><h2>Designing while the<br/><em>service was evolving.</em></h2><p>Changing service requirements, system rules and technical discoveries continuously affected the journey. Design had to balance delivery momentum with an experience that felt intuitive, transparent and trustworthy.</p><blockquote>We needed to stop designing around how the service worked and start designing around how people understood it.</blockquote></div></div><div class="case-row"><p class="eyebrow">03 / MY CONTRIBUTION</p><div><h2>Creating clarity.<br/><em>Building alignment.</em></h2><p>I owned the UX across key Global Pay journeys on app and desktop, while taking on broader design leadership responsibilities.</p><ul class="contributions"><li>Facilitated cross-functional workshops to align people, rules and dependencies.</li><li>Mapped complex service rules and flows to reveal the customer experience.</li><li>Led usability testing and advocated for experience improvements.</li><li>Introduced processes to track design changes, CX implications and delivery impact.</li></ul></div></div></section><section class="journey section-pad"><p class="eyebrow">04 / THE EXPERIENCE</p><h2>The right information.<br/><em>At the right moment.</em></h2><div class="journey-grid">{#each [{title:'Add a beneficiary',text:'Capture and verify beneficiary details with clear guidance.'},{title:'Select a payment method',text:'Show relevant payment methods based on destination and beneficiary.'},{title:'Enter an amount',text:'Make fees, FX rates and the total cost transparent upfront.'},{title:'Review and confirm',text:'Give customers visibility and control, with clear confirmation.'}] as step,i}<article><span class="step-number">0{i+1}</span><h3>{step.title}</h3><p>{step.text}</p></article>{/each}</div></section><section class="case-body section-pad"><div class="case-row"><p class="eyebrow">05 / LEARNING & IMPACT</p><div><h2>Confidence comes<br/><em>from understanding.</em></h2><p>Testing revealed uncertainty around unfamiliar international payment concepts. Those insights informed changes to beneficiary selection and saving, indicative indicators, and the presentation of fees and VAT.</p><p>The work established a foundation for a more competitive, customer-led cross-border payments offering. Cross-functional alignment helped the team navigate changing capabilities, evolving APIs and delivery dependencies.</p></div></div></section><section class="closing section-pad"><p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE FURTHER</p><h2>Design meets strategy.<br/><em>Performance with purpose.</em></h2><a class="button light" href="/#work">Back to selected work <span>↖</span></a></section></main>
